Merge two arrays
Problem statement

Combine two arrays into one array.

Input:
int[] a = {1, 2, 3};
int[] b = {4, 5, 6};


Expected output:
[1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6]


A. Approach and logic
The result array needs enough capacity for both arrays.

Required size:
\[
a.length+b.length
\]

Copy the first array into the beginning of the result and the second array into the remaining positions.
B. Plain Java
int[] a = {1, 2, 3};
int[] b = {4, 5, 6};

int[] result = new int[a.length + b.length];

for (int i = 0; i < a.length; i++) {
    result[i] = a[i];
}

for (int i = 0; i < b.length; i++) {
    result[a.length + i] = b[i];
}

System.out.println(Arrays.toString(result));


C. Java 8
int[] a = {1, 2, 3};
int[] b = {4, 5, 6};

int[] result = IntStream.concat(
        Arrays.stream(a),
        Arrays.stream(b)
).toArray();

System.out.println(Arrays.toString(result));


D. Complexity
Let the first array have n elements and the second have m elements.
Solution	Time	Extra space
Plain Java	O(n + m)	O(n + m)
Java 8	O(n + m)	O(n + m)

E. Choice of solution
Both approaches are optimal because every element must be copied into the resulting array.











Merge two sorted arrays into one sorted array
Problem statement

Given two already sorted arrays, merge them into a single sorted array.

Input:
int[] a = {1, 3, 5};
int[] b = {2, 4, 6};


Expected output:
[1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6]


A. Approach and logic
Since both arrays are already sorted, we don't need to sort the final result again.

Use two pointers:
- i tracks the first array.
- j tracks the second array.
- k tracks the result array.
Compare a[i] and b[j].

Place the smaller element into the result and advance its pointer.

Continue until one array is exhausted. Copy the remaining elements from the other array.
B. Plain Java
int[] a = {1, 3, 5};
int[] b = {2, 4, 6};

int[] result = new int[a.length + b.length];

int i = 0, j = 0, k = 0;

while (i < a.length && j < b.length) {

    if (a[i] <= b[j]) {
        result[k++] = a[i++];
    } else {
        result[k++] = b[j++];
    }
}

while (i < a.length) {
    result[k++] = a[i++];
}

while (j < b.length) {
    result[k++] = b[j++];
}

System.out.println(Arrays.toString(result));


C. Dry run
Comparison	Selected value	Result
1 vs 2	1	1
3 vs 2	2	1, 2
3 vs 4	3	1, 2, 3
5 vs 4	4	1, 2, 3, 4
5 vs 6	5	1, 2, 3, 4, 5
Remaining	6	1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6

D. Java 8
int[] a = {1, 3, 5};
int[] b = {2, 4, 6};

int[] result = IntStream.concat(
        Arrays.stream(a),
        Arrays.stream(b)
).sorted()
 .toArray();

System.out.println(Arrays.toString(result));


E. Complexity
Let total elements be N = n + m.
Solution	Time	Extra space
Two pointers	O(n + m)	O(n + m)
Java 8 concat + sorted	O(N log N)	O(N)

F. Choice of solution
Choose the two-pointer approach.

This is an important interview distinction: when input arrays are already sorted, take advantage of their ordering instead of sorting everything again.
